DECISION
AR-H133 is POSITIVE
for:
CHEAP REPRESENTATION-DEBT MARKERS

WITH:
A HARD MARKER-COST LIMIT.

Supported synthetically:

- current low-value interactions can become future high-value interactions;
- full forgetting loses most future-important residuals;
- fully storing every suspicious interaction is too expensive;
- cheap dormant markers strongly improve future rediscovery;
- marker-driven re-audition captures most future semantic value;
- marker state itself must remain memory-priced;
- representation debt provides an intermediate state between full model and total forgetting.

Not demonstrated:

- marker compaction across related residuals;
- marker corruption / stale provenance;
- many-phase marker accumulation;
- marker scheduling under shared challenge budgets;
- interaction between representation debt and hypothesis ecology;
- real grammar reconstruction.

